  <section>
    <h3>Safe exploration and evaluation of new AI tools</h3>
    <p>To support learning, development and innovation, we do encourage safe exploration and evaluation of new AI tools. However, this must be done in accordance with our IT policies, within a controlled process with clear evaluation criteria, and e.g. only use data already available on the public internet, not corporate or NHS data (this includes your own notes and documents).</p>
    <p>For example:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Researchers compared the performance of ChatGPT vs. other LLMs for extracting data from interviews. The data used for the purpose of this evaluation were case studies already available on the public internet.</li>
      <li>A designer needed some artwork to include in an external presentation. They used general prompts that did not contain any corporate data to ask the AI to generate suitable background images. They also checked with the AI that it did not break any Copyright issues before using it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How to choose an AI tool?</h3>
    <p>AI models are trained using data. AI models can suffer from data leakage, depending on their intended mode of operation.</p>
    <p>The <a href="https://www.govx.digital/hubfs/AI_Playbook_for_the_UK_Government__12_02_.pdf.pdf" target="_blank">Artificial Intelligence Playbook for the UK government</a> recommends asking the following key questions when deciding which AI tool to use:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Where is your data being sent and how is it being processed?</li>
      <li>Is your data being used to train future models?</li>
      <li>How long is your data being retained?</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Using a new tool</h2>
    <p>Before implementing or trialling any product, or if you want to procure enterprise licenses for an AI tool, you need to follow the new <a href="https://nhs.sharepoint.com/sites/X26_URPG/SitePages/request-new-tooling-software.aspx?source=https%3a//nhs.sharepoint.com/sites/X26_URPG/SitePages/Forms/AllPages.aspx" target="_blank">software request process.</a> Getting new software approved, especially if it is paid, will likely involve quite a lengthy process which includes getting approval from:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Getting Approval To Spend (GATS)</li>
      <li>Business Case (Financial and Commercial)</li>
      <li>Information Governance</li>
      <li>Cyber-security</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Tools that are not Approved</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>All tools used for NHS work</strong> must be approved by Information Governance (IG), Cybersecurity, and Finance, to ensure NHSE compliance and security.</li>
      <li>This is not an exhaustive list. Tools that are not listed should not be considered automatically approved. We have focused on the most common tools that are relevant to research and design.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>ChatGPT (OpenAI)</h3>
    <h4>ChatGPT (free)</h4>
    <p>You cannot use the free version of ChatGPT when doing any type of NHSE-related work. The tool is not supported by NHS IT. There is currently a lack of transparency over how data is being used or stored. Unless you opt out, inputted data will be used to train future versions of the model.</p>
    <h4>ChatGPT Enterprise (paid)</h4>
    <p>The Enterprise version is more secure compared to the free version. It includes Enterprise-level security and controls, System for Cross-domain Identity Management, user analytics, domain verification, and role-based access controls.</p>
    <p>However, there are currently no Enterprise licenses available in NHSE for ChatGPT. If you want to procure Enterprise licenses you need to follow the new software request process.</p>

    <h3>NotebookLM (Google)</h3>
    <p>Grounds its responses only in the documents, web pages, or videos you upload. It highlights exact references in your materials, showing where information came from. It should therefore avoid hallucinations by sticking strictly to your content.</p>
    <h4>NotebookLM (free)</h4>
    <p>You cannot use the free version of NotebookLM when doing any type of NHSE-related work. The tool is not supported by NHS IT. NotebookLM does not use personal data to train the Gemini model.</p>
    <h4>NotebookLM for enterprise (paid) - Via procurement process</h4>
    <p>The Enterprise version is more secure compared to the free version. It includes additional privacy and security features like Virtual Private Cloud Service Controls to protect sensitive data from unauthorized access or data exfiltration. It also includes Identity and Access Management controls which can make sure that the right people have the right access at the right time.</p>
    <p>However, there are currently no Enterprise licenses available in NHSE for NotebookLM.</p>

    <h3>Microsoft Clipchamp</h3>
    <p>Clipchamp is currently not permitted for use because it includes AI-powered features, and using it would involve sharing personal data with an AI service. If you need to edit a video, you can use DaVinci Resolve, which is available through NHSE's Company Portal.</p>

    <h3>Mural AI</h3>
    <p>Mural is an approved whiteboard tool in NHSE, together with Microsoft Whiteboard. Mural has some incorporated AI features. However, these AI features are not currently available in NHSE.</p>

    <h3>GitHub CoPilot</h3>
    <p>Personal paid accounts are not approved due to data security risks and insufficient enterprise safeguards. See process for requesting an <a href="http://localhost:3000/artificial-intelligence/using-ai-to-support-design#how-to-prototype-in-code">enterprise GitHub Copilot account.</a></p>

    <h3>Claude.ai</h3>
    <p>Personal paid accounts are not approved due to data security risks and insufficient enterprise safeguards.</p>

  </section>
